I've had the opportunity to meet a lot  of people who volunteer to wear a  uniform DoD and I've learned so much  about that culture you know most of us  have colleagues and co-workers and they  have brothers and sisters it's it's a  real and different connection and  because the stakes are so much higher  the willingness to serve to be there for  each other means something as simple as  having each other's backs it doesn't  mean they necessarily like each other or  get along but they always have each  other's backs and I find this  astonishing I had the chance to go to  Afghanistan as a guest of the Air Force  they wanted me to go there and see them  perform their mission and then come back  and the deal was to go back to  headquarters and to simply report what I  had seen to share my insights if there  was anything for them to learn or  anything that they could glean now  everything on my trip went wrong ten  minutes after we landed in Bagram the  base came on a rocket attack the plane  that I was supposed to fly home on we  ended up not being able to fly home on  and we thought we were gonna get stuck  in Afghanistan and the plane that I did  eventually fly home on was not a plane  we were scheduled to fly home on and we  would bring home a flag draped casket  just me and my two escorts were the only  three passengers abort this c-17 cargo  plane with a single flag draped casket  strapped down in the middle of the cargo  bay we flew with we brought him home  flew with him for nine and a half hours  from from Bagram to Ramstein in Germany  this experience changed me it had a  profound impact on me but it was when I  got back home I still had to fulfill my  responsibilities and go back and report  on what I had seen and what I had  learned I went out to the Air Force Base  and all the brass from Mobility Command  was there all general officers and I  told what I had learned that I had  shared what I had seen and I got to the  point where I was gonna tell the story  of the fat that last flight home  and I choked up and I wasn't sure I  could make it through the story and I  started and there's no way if I had kept  talking I would have lost it and I just  stood there completely choked up now if  this was the private sector  somebody would have said don't worry  somebody would have said take your time  but that's not what was said as I stood  there not being able to speak one lone  voice spoke up and he said go on the  underlying message was you're safe the  underlying message was we got your back  you're OK in the private sector we  actually encourage people to hold back  and he was telling me move forwards and  it reminded me of my friend Johnny Quest  who said this is what you signed up for  you have to do this the underlying  message was I will be there with you and  I will be there for you
 

